1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,120
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:13,540
You know, if you've never been to Alaska, you really need to go one of these days if you can possibly manage them.

3
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:17,660
I mean, it's a stunning place. I remember when I was up there years and years and years ago,

4
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:24,420
we crested this ridge as we were hiking through a park and we looked over and there were four grizzly bears.

5
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:32,740
They were a good distance away, but there were four grizzly bears kind of standing up on their hind legs, looking off into the distance.

6
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:41,200
And my buddy who I was hiking with at the time, he says, wow, I wonder what that's all about.

7
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:43,920
And I said, hmm, bears watching.

8
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:47,100
Bears.

9
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,440
Oh, all right. Well, this is podcast number 38.

10
00:00:54,420 --> 00:01:24,400
Hey, welcome back to the show.

11
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:25,340
Scoutmaster podcast.

12
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:27,120
This is Clark Green.

13
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:30,400
Here's a couple of emails I wanted to respond to very quickly.

14
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,580
Robert from Richmond, Virginia wrote.

15
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:37,480
He said, I wanted to let you know that I really enjoy your podcast.

16
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:39,320
I have a question I'm hoping you can answer.

17
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:43,240
I remember a song that I can't get out of my head.

18
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:46,340
It was something along the lines of he's my all time favorite Boy Scout.

19
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:47,880
They say it over and over again.

20
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,740
All time Boy Scout.

21
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,140
You're my favorite all time Boy Scout.

22
00:01:54,179 --> 00:01:56,340
So that's the one I think you were thinking about.

23
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,960
And I wanted to play it at a training session.

24
00:01:59,039 --> 00:02:01,899
Well, I sent Robert a link to that song.

25
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:02,860
Thanks, Robert.

26
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:04,880
Phil wrote.

27
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:09,020
You know that every time I hear the opening of your show, I laugh.

28
00:02:09,419 --> 00:02:11,140
Well, Phil, you might be on your own there.

29
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,740
I'm sure this is Phil.

30
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,520
I'm sure you've received all kinds of hate mail to stop telling jokes.

31
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:21,100
I haven't gotten any hate mail yet.

32
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:24,640
But there's always a first time.

33
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:27,600
But I'm saying don't stop.

34
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:29,260
Actually, I'm writing to ask you a favor.

35
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,340
Would you possibly have the one about the brains written down in your notes?

36
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:37,140
And if you would do that, would you be willing to send me a copy?

37
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:40,220
I'd love to use it as an opener for our district roundtable.

38
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:43,820
So there was somebody looking for a joke and a song that they wanted to use

39
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:45,500
in a training session or a roundtable.

40
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,240
And I'm very happy to lead you to where those are.

41
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,520
But you're not allowed to tell them that you got it from me.

42
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:51,500
Please.

43
00:02:52,120 --> 00:02:53,100
I might get hate mail.

44
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:57,700
No, it's always nice to hear from everybody.

45
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:05,360
You can contact me at ClarkGreen, C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N, at gmail.com.

46
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,720
So this time around in the Scoutmaster podcast, we have an interview.

47
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:12,100
And it's going to take up the entirety of the podcast.

48
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,440
It's an interview with Larry Geiger.

49
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:17,500
And Larry is a Scoutmaster down in Cocoa, Florida.

50
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:19,560
Cocoa, Florida.

51
00:03:20,520 --> 00:03:21,079
Oh.

52
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:23,079
Kids?

53
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:26,359
Kids, can you turn the TV off?

54
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:28,040
Because I'm trying to record.

55
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:29,200
Yeah.

56
00:03:30,079 --> 00:03:30,800
Thank you.

57
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:35,380
Larry's been following the blog and the podcast for a while.

58
00:03:35,540 --> 00:03:39,300
And he writes in frequently with comments and things.

59
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:45,340
And I thought it might be a good idea to get him on the internets and interview him.

60
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:50,740
Because it's important for us to hear from other people other than me.

61
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:51,740
Right?

62
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:54,700
I mean, Scouting is a great thing.

63
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:56,160
And there's a lot of people involved with it.

64
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:02,580
It's good to hear success stories from people like Larry and his troop down there in Cocoa, Florida.

65
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:05,360
So we're going to hear that in just a moment.

66
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:10,120
Where does that keep coming from?

67
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:11,220
I thought it was the TV.

68
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:12,200
Hey.

69
00:04:12,420 --> 00:04:12,980
Oh, yeah.

70
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:13,920
I Dream of Jeannie.

71
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:14,860
Cocoa, Florida.

72
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:15,640
Cocoa Beach.

73
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:18,300
That's where the show was set.

74
00:04:18,459 --> 00:04:21,360
And just in case you didn't make the connection the first time.

75
00:04:22,099 --> 00:04:22,280
Yeah.

76
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:26,200
The interview runs for about 20 minutes or so.

77
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:30,440
I hope you enjoy it as much as I enjoyed talking with Larry.

78
00:04:31,039 --> 00:04:31,700
Hey, here we go.

79
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:32,800
Let's get started, shall we?

80
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:38,180
I'm speaking with Larry Geiger, who is a frequent contributor to the comments on the Scoutmaster blog,

81
00:04:38,719 --> 00:04:42,380
a listener to the Scoutmaster podcast, and a scouter.

82
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:43,580
How are you doing tonight, Larry?

83
00:04:44,419 --> 00:04:44,900
Doing good.

84
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:47,760
So Cocoa, Florida.

85
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:53,320
You know that Cocoa, Florida, it brings back like I Dream of Jeannie and Cocoa Beach.

86
00:04:53,539 --> 00:04:54,099
Am I right?

87
00:04:54,520 --> 00:05:00,539
Cocoa Beach is right across the river on the island side, on the Cocoa Beach side.

88
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:01,460
I'm on the mainland.

89
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:02,560
All right.

90
00:05:02,599 --> 00:05:06,240
So I have dated myself by mentioning an old television show.

91
00:05:06,719 --> 00:05:07,880
And Ron John's Surf Shop.

92
00:05:08,479 --> 00:05:09,200
Oh, right.

93
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:11,680
Gives us all some kind of an idea of where you're at.

94
00:05:12,159 --> 00:05:14,780
And what troop is it that you work with down there?

95
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:18,299
I'm with troop 720 right now.

96
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:18,799
720.

97
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:21,619
And about how many fellows in that troop now?

98
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:22,380
25.

99
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:24,280
And what's your role?

100
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:25,780
I'm the Scoutmaster.

101
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:28,700
Give me an idea of how you ended up doing that.

102
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:32,219
Well, I've been working with a troop for about three years.

103
00:05:33,659 --> 00:05:35,300
Pastor asked me to help out.

104
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:36,440
They were a small troop.

105
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:38,740
And so I was helping out.

106
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:44,660
And two years ago in October, the Scoutmaster resigned to take over the district chairmanship.

107
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:47,240
And they asked me.

108
00:05:47,580 --> 00:05:48,620
And you said?

109
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:49,200
Okay.

110
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:49,960
Okay.

111
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:55,359
I suppose if nobody else will do it, I'll go ahead and do it.

112
00:05:55,419 --> 00:05:57,500
I think that's the way most of us got the job there.

113
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:01,960
Well, I've been Scoutmaster for 10 years in another troop.

114
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:02,359
Aha.

115
00:06:02,919 --> 00:06:06,179
And I had retired from that and done some other stuff.

116
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,640
And so here I am back.

117
00:06:08,979 --> 00:06:12,060
As a Scoutmaster, what are your biggest challenges?

118
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:16,240
Well, the biggest challenge, obviously, is the one we all have, which is leadership.

119
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:31,020
We kind of have an idea in our mind where we want the older Scouts to go with teaching and training and actually being the leaders, the patrol leaders and the SPL.

120
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:37,580
And so sometimes we have a picture where we think that ought to go and they don't go there.

121
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:42,740
But we keep working on that.

122
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,400
And sometimes it turns out really, really well.

123
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:49,960
So you've been at this with this troop and another one for about 13 years.

124
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:50,880
Yes.

125
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:58,080
What changed between year one and year 13 in the way that you approached being a Scoutmaster?

126
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:01,960
I think the main thing is confidence in the program.

127
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:09,120
When you're a young leader, I think if you've got a lot of ideas, part of that is wanting to change things.

128
00:07:10,099 --> 00:07:12,000
And I like what Ask Andy says.

129
00:07:12,340 --> 00:07:15,340
He constantly goes back to read the manual, read the book.

130
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:15,940
Right.

131
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:17,359
He says that over and over again.

132
00:07:17,659 --> 00:07:19,060
Well, as you do.

133
00:07:20,060 --> 00:07:21,640
You know, read the program.

134
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:26,560
And after a while, you begin to figure out that that is what works.

135
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:36,120
I get a lot of feedback and comments from people who are kind of in those first three or four years of working with Scouting.

136
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:45,600
And they get really frustrated with the picture of the program as it's represented in training and in a lot of the literature and things.

137
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:49,740
And the reality of what they're actually dealing with.

138
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:55,060
You're telling me the longer you go on, the more you trust that that's sound and solid.

139
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:56,800
Yes, absolutely.

140
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:01,300
Over the time you've spent as Scoutmaster, do you see the same problems all the time?

141
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:02,600
Pretty much.

142
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:13,340
It's bringing the young guys up through the program, getting them to first class, and then turning them back around to look back and to bring the new younger guys up.

143
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:16,640
I mean, that's what you're doing, cycling through that over and over again.

144
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:39,880
And as certain groups move out of your troop, you know, as they get up to 16, 17 years old, sometimes if you don't have the filler in the middle, if you've lost that for whatever reason, the guys came in but they didn't stay or you had a bad recruiting year, then you're back to a bunch of young guys.

145
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:43,480
And it cycles, you're right, it cycles like that.

146
00:08:44,179 --> 00:08:51,420
What would you consider to be the most important elements to have as a Scout leader to ensure the success of your Scouts?

147
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:54,160
The first thing is I'm not in charge.

148
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:57,160
I'm not in charge of anything.

149
00:08:57,699 --> 00:09:01,100
Maybe I'm in charge of training the SPL, but that's it, nothing else.

150
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:04,560
The patrol leaders are in charge of everything the committee doesn't do.

151
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:07,620
The committee chairman is in charge of all the business.

152
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:10,600
You know, basically it's about the patrol leaders.

153
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:15,800
The second thing that I've learned is scouting does not happen in meetings.

154
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:20,660
I tell people, particularly new parents over and over again, it happens out there.

155
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:26,339
Actually, what happens in the meetings is not very important to some extent.

156
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:30,160
And some people are going to start to quiver when I say something like that.

157
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:33,339
I am not quivering at all, by the way.

158
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:33,620
Okay.

159
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:35,180
It happens out in the woods.

160
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:36,540
It happens on the camp out.

161
00:09:36,839 --> 00:09:39,079
And that's where scouting really, really happens.

162
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:40,180
Now, they need the meetings.

163
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:43,680
But, you know, football doesn't happen in the locker room.

164
00:09:43,719 --> 00:09:44,939
It happens out on the field.

165
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:45,400
Right.

166
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:46,900
I'll say this.

167
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:51,980
Boy Scouts are almost universally unable to run a great scout meeting.

168
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:59,520
You know, a 14-year-old kid is just not quite ready to run what a lot of adults would consider an ideal meeting.

169
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:00,800
That's okay.

170
00:10:01,459 --> 00:10:02,780
There's nothing wrong with that.

171
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:04,380
But that scares a lot of people.

172
00:10:04,819 --> 00:10:07,260
You mentioned one of my favorite words, which is chaos.

173
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:09,540
And to me, chaos is bad.

174
00:10:10,079 --> 00:10:11,079
Energy is good.

175
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:11,880
Noise is good.

176
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:13,260
Chaos is bad.

177
00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:19,040
So basically, as a leader, it's our job to put a lid on the chaos.

178
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:20,400
Now, what does that mean then?

179
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:29,420
Signs of chaos, hazing, bullying, no uniforms, sloppy uniforms, lack of advancements, lack of older scouts working with younger kids.

180
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:41,120
And then things that bring that about, lack of training, lack of skill development, selfishness, laziness, lack of direction, all the things that describe boys sometimes.

181
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:42,100
Right.

182
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:47,440
But the energy and the noise, we had a fairly noisy meeting Tuesday night.

183
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:55,820
And it's amazing to me how quiet it can be on a camp out, even when they're kind of around a campfire.

184
00:10:55,960 --> 00:10:58,040
And yet in the meeting, it sounds like they're all nuts.

185
00:10:58,860 --> 00:10:59,040
Yeah.

186
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:00,960
Actually, I never thought of it that way.

187
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:01,560
But you're right.

188
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:04,060
That's a very interesting juxtaposition.

189
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:04,780
Yeah.

190
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:06,980
And then there's the leadership.

191
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,480
We're running an experiment.

192
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:10,520
Uh-huh.

193
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:14,080
We create an environment where they can practice leadership.

194
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:15,820
And I guess this is the key to it.

195
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:18,020
I tell my guys over and over and over again.

196
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:21,540
I tell the leaders in the troop, what's your job?

197
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:24,360
And they say, well, SPL or I'm supposed to do this.

198
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:30,220
No, your job is to lead, train, and inspire scouts to attain first class rank.

199
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:30,780
Uh-huh.

200
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:39,540
And after a while, if you came to my troop meeting and asked my older scouts, they would just right off the bat say, my job is to lead, train, and inspire.

201
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:46,880
And then I ask, well, I say, okay, if you're in charge of their advancement, then who's in charge of Star Life and Eagle advancement?

202
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:47,580
Uh-huh.

203
00:11:47,580 --> 00:11:50,240
They kind of look at me the first couple times.

204
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:52,000
And then I tell them, well, you are.

205
00:11:52,580 --> 00:11:55,120
You're in charge of your own Star Life and Eagle.

206
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:58,260
And then they kind of look around and go, wait, I'm in charge of everything.

207
00:11:58,640 --> 00:11:58,960
Uh-huh.

208
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:00,000
Da-da.

209
00:12:00,340 --> 00:12:00,760
Bingo.

210
00:12:01,340 --> 00:12:01,640
Bingo.

211
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:01,740
Yeah.

212
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:03,380
And that's how it works.

213
00:12:03,780 --> 00:12:06,320
That's – I just keep saying that.

214
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:07,520
And some of them get it.

215
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:18,480
Now, this troop that you're working with now, when you took it on three years ago, it's three years, right?

216
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:19,660
Two years.

217
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:20,460
Two years ago.

218
00:12:20,699 --> 00:12:20,920
Okay.

219
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:25,420
Was it operating the way that you wanted it to operate?

220
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:26,699
No.

221
00:12:26,699 --> 00:12:33,360
The previous leaders had a bit of frustration because of what we just talked about.

222
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:33,880
Uh-huh.

223
00:12:33,880 --> 00:12:34,320
Um-huh.

224
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:41,380
The – some of what was going on, they just didn't feel like they were getting to where they wanted to go.

225
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:42,000
Uh-huh.

226
00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:43,220
It wasn't quiet.

227
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:44,340
It wasn't a classroom.

228
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:48,180
It wasn't – and I think after a while that kind of frustrated them.

229
00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:50,520
And I guess – I don't know.

230
00:12:50,580 --> 00:12:56,439
I'm one of those people that I have a very – the noise and all the other stuff, you know, it doesn't bother me.

231
00:12:57,060 --> 00:12:57,180
Well –

232
00:12:57,180 --> 00:13:00,240
I have very high tolerance for just the energy.

233
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:06,140
From an adult perspective, things look noisy and chaotic and crazy.

234
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:10,760
And from a boy perspective, they look just about right.

235
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:11,820
Right.

236
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:12,340
Exactly.

237
00:13:12,340 --> 00:13:22,400
So you described when you take – took over this troop, some frustrated leadership who had maybe a different vision and a picture of scouting than their scouts did.

238
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:28,420
Because there's another 20 guys listening to us right now who are doing exactly what you did two years ago.

239
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:29,860
Got some really great ideas.

240
00:13:30,220 --> 00:13:31,820
They've been asked to be a scoutmaster.

241
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:35,740
And they step into the middle of a situation that's not optimal.

242
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:37,120
It might not be falling apart.

243
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:39,680
They really want to make some changes and do some things.

244
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:41,400
You've got their ear now, Larry.

245
00:13:41,479 --> 00:13:42,339
Tell them what to do.

246
00:13:42,520 --> 00:13:45,120
Well, it's not exactly by the book.

247
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:53,160
But I found a venue down in Sebastian, a restaurant that had a really nice conference meeting, conference room.

248
00:13:53,479 --> 00:13:55,520
And I planned a day of training.

249
00:13:56,180 --> 00:14:01,780
And like I said, what I try to do is treat the guys like CEOs and vice presidents.

250
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:03,900
I want a really nice room.

251
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:05,140
I want a nice lunch.

252
00:14:05,199 --> 00:14:06,240
I want projectors.

253
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:07,040
I want sound.

254
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:14,300
We sat down and we talked about lead, train, and inspire 14 times and a bunch of other stuff.

255
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:19,680
And we told them what the committee's job was and told them that here it is.

256
00:14:19,699 --> 00:14:20,199
It's yours.

257
00:14:20,620 --> 00:14:22,760
So in May, we have an annual planning conference.

258
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:30,719
And we have a schedule October through the next July, including where we're going to go to camp, every month's campout, every program.

259
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:32,820
They need a structure.

260
00:14:34,060 --> 00:14:40,000
Just kind of flailing from month to month doesn't build that kind of leadership that you want.

261
00:14:40,560 --> 00:14:51,240
When they know that in January and February we're going backpacking, they know that in December for a meeting and one or two meetings in January, they've got to teach the new guys how to backpack.

262
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:52,960
And it's in the calendar.

263
00:14:53,220 --> 00:14:54,080
We're going to do it.

264
00:14:54,080 --> 00:14:56,860
We're going because we don't change our calendar very often.

265
00:14:57,260 --> 00:15:00,120
In the last two years, we've changed one date by a week.

266
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:01,620
But they know it's coming.

267
00:15:02,260 --> 00:15:07,260
And there's a schedule pressure there that they can understand.

268
00:15:07,560 --> 00:15:12,740
They know by that Tuesday night before the campout, if they've got kids that aren't ready, there's going to be problems.

269
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:17,380
They make the schedule, and then I keep giving it back to them.

270
00:15:17,440 --> 00:15:20,100
Guys, are you ready for this?

271
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:20,960
Are you ready for this?

272
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:21,720
Are you ready for this?

273
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:25,020
And boys tend to be lazy.

274
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:26,900
They tend to wait until the last minute.

275
00:15:27,100 --> 00:15:28,980
They tend to procrastinate.

276
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:29,480
Yeah.

277
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:32,580
But when they know it's coming, they've got to be ready.

278
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:38,880
You know, they do think about it, and they do get ready, and they do learn from not being prepared.

279
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:42,980
And then my next question to you was, now, do you let them forget to plan?

280
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:43,640
Oh, yeah.

281
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:47,520
They do it every week to some extent.

282
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:49,960
In August, the committee approves the plan.

283
00:15:49,960 --> 00:15:54,360
The SPL comes in, and the patrol leaders, they present it to the committee.

284
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:57,560
Everybody in the troop knows what's coming up.

285
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:00,200
Scouting for food, campouts.

286
00:16:00,740 --> 00:16:03,240
You know, nobody can come back and say, wait.

287
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,940
You know, I hand out schedules all year round.

288
00:16:06,060 --> 00:16:10,120
I keep a pile of 30 of them in my notebook, and I just hand them out to people all the time.

289
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:14,200
So there's no communication issue about, you know, hey, we didn't know.

290
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:19,860
And then each month, the program revolves around that.

291
00:16:20,060 --> 00:16:25,240
Now, they know that, well, here it is, August, September, I'm underfoot.

292
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:27,420
December, I'm second class.

293
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:30,340
Maybe by May, I'll be first class or almost done.

294
00:16:30,340 --> 00:16:34,260
And the scouts have those goals in their book.

295
00:16:34,940 --> 00:16:39,720
And, you know, they're pushing the older scouts a little bit.

296
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:42,620
And the older scouts are pushing them a little bit.

297
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:45,660
And it's not how I would do it, but it's kind of happening.

298
00:16:46,660 --> 00:16:49,000
They don't do it the way that you would do it.

299
00:16:49,120 --> 00:16:49,320
No.

300
00:16:49,320 --> 00:16:54,360
How does a person keep that from just driving them absolutely up a wall?

301
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:56,920
Oh, well, it doesn't bother me that much.

302
00:16:57,240 --> 00:16:58,660
Like I said, I like the energy.

303
00:16:58,940 --> 00:17:01,840
I have a lot of investment in them doing it.

304
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:08,980
It's really, really important for me that they grow into men through this process and that they do it.

305
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:18,080
But it's not how I or my committee chairman or, you know, some of the other leaders, not how we would run the meeting.

306
00:17:19,319 --> 00:17:22,379
You know, they run over the sections of the meeting.

307
00:17:22,540 --> 00:17:24,260
You know, it's supposed to be 20 minutes for this.

308
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:26,500
And they go 30 or they go three.

309
00:17:26,639 --> 00:17:27,919
And then they go play a game.

310
00:17:28,040 --> 00:17:34,159
And, you know, I mean, it's a lot more random, energetic, loud.

311
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:35,120
Right.

312
00:17:35,540 --> 00:17:38,340
Well, that takes, I mean, that takes tremendous patience.

313
00:17:38,540 --> 00:17:39,780
It takes a little bit, yes.

314
00:17:39,780 --> 00:17:41,060
Let me make a guess.

315
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:47,540
The patience comes from a confidence in the program, the way that scouts respond to it.

316
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:48,840
Yes.

317
00:17:49,399 --> 00:17:53,919
And you've gotten to see the outcome a few times in the past.

318
00:17:54,560 --> 00:17:56,540
Now, that helps a lot.

319
00:17:56,879 --> 00:17:57,080
Yeah.

320
00:17:57,100 --> 00:17:59,439
And you have to have some kind of memory.

321
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:01,540
I have a scout.

322
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:02,800
His name's Paul.

323
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:05,820
Paul hiked the entire Appalachian Trail in a season.

324
00:18:06,820 --> 00:18:12,320
Paul was a little tiny 11-year-old, almost quit after his first camp out.

325
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:14,460
We almost killed him.

326
00:18:15,639 --> 00:18:16,399
Not really.

327
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:18,720
But it was very wet, very cold.

328
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:19,960
It was a backpacking trip.

329
00:18:20,179 --> 00:18:22,300
And he was a very small 11-year-old.

330
00:18:22,300 --> 00:18:25,919
But, you know, he used to run around, make a lot of noise.

331
00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:29,840
You know, he's now got a family with three kids.

332
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:32,940
He's a successful guy thriving on his own.

333
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:37,060
And he's gone from dependence to independence.

334
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:37,860
Mm-hmm.

335
00:18:37,860 --> 00:18:45,179
And every once in a while, I have to look around the room and picture that kid at 28.

336
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:55,500
I want my guys to be grown, to have families, to be independent, and to achieve the things in life that they want.

337
00:18:55,620 --> 00:19:00,540
I really, really, truly believe scouting can help a lot of guys get there.

338
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:01,980
I've seen it.

339
00:19:02,380 --> 00:19:05,680
I want my wife to receive a lot of wedding and baby shower invitations.

340
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:16,740
You know, I could talk about, you know, 38 Eagle Scouts and what they're doing now and where they've gone in their lives.

341
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:21,740
And my wife has gotten a lot of wedding invitations and shower invitations.

342
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:35,340
And you have to look at the guys in the room right now that are 11 and 12 years old that are, at that very second, running around totally nuts and project that.

343
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:39,980
And I guess with time, it does become a little bit easier.

344
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:45,960
Was there a point where you doubted whether or not this was actually going to work?

345
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:57,160
I think early on, yeah, there were times, I know, I guess to some extent, not totally because it worked for me.

346
00:19:57,880 --> 00:20:00,800
I joined scouting on my 11th birthday.

347
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:08,320
And from that day on, almost every single week, I was at a scout meeting until my 18th birthday.

348
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:14,340
So I went to college and almost every month for seven years, I was camping.

349
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:17,520
And I know what that meant to me.

350
00:20:18,179 --> 00:20:22,240
And so I knew that was going to mean the same thing to some of the guys.

351
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:30,399
It has been surprising sometimes the guys that I didn't think it was going to mean anything to, and it has.

352
00:20:30,939 --> 00:20:31,080
Yeah.

353
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:38,840
So I have more confidence now that that's true for more guys than I would have guessed a while back.

354
00:20:38,840 --> 00:20:44,480
You know that the program works, but you can't really predict exactly how it's all going to come out.

355
00:20:44,879 --> 00:20:51,340
What happens in those seven years is really, truly amazing in a boy's life, a young man's life.

356
00:20:51,860 --> 00:20:56,179
It's, for most guys, it's a very, very large change.

357
00:20:56,180 --> 00:21:02,680
They're mommy's little boy, and they're almost a man at the other end.

358
00:21:02,780 --> 00:21:09,620
What do you see happening within the next three or four years within your troop and your work as a scout leader?

359
00:21:09,620 --> 00:21:15,760
Well, I've got all these little guys, and I've got a group of about four or five older scouts.

360
00:21:16,459 --> 00:21:26,780
And I'm kind of in a hurry to get these guys up to first class because I need some leaders because I've got a whole bunch of more Weeblos, maybe another 15 to 20.

361
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:27,399
Wow.

362
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:31,580
So we could grow again another couple of patrols.

363
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:47,940
So I need some first class scouts to be ready to take on their leadership roles and start working on their Star Life and Eagle, which includes that requirement for leadership, which is lead, train, and inspire scouts to first class.

364
00:21:48,060 --> 00:21:48,880
Wait, did I say it again?

365
00:21:48,980 --> 00:21:49,420
I'm sorry.

366
00:21:49,420 --> 00:21:54,560
You have a pretty good – I mean, you answered that question very readily.

367
00:21:54,920 --> 00:21:56,440
You have a long view.

368
00:21:57,620 --> 00:22:02,040
You're looking three and four moves ahead and seeing what's going to happen.

369
00:22:03,940 --> 00:22:05,040
What might happen.

370
00:22:05,580 --> 00:22:08,000
Well, what could happen, right.

371
00:22:08,040 --> 00:22:09,720
What might happen, you know.

372
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:22,800
I think that couples into this kind of formula for success is having that longer view and being able to look past what's happening at a troop meeting or on a campout or something like that.

373
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:27,880
Well, another thing I do is every two years I like to have a Hyatt venture trip.

374
00:22:28,360 --> 00:22:36,480
I've told the current senior patrol leader I'm kind of looking for him when he's done to start a venture patrol.

375
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:37,180
Mm-hmm.

376
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:42,720
I've said in their minds summer 2012, I don't tell them what to do, but I'll give them ideas.

377
00:22:43,380 --> 00:22:52,560
You know, I'm not telling them what to do or even when to do it, but I'll tell them some opportunities that – you know, some ideas if they can't think of anything.

378
00:22:52,880 --> 00:22:57,120
There's got to be some kind of reciprocal initiative on their part for this to happen.

379
00:22:57,560 --> 00:22:57,800
Right.

380
00:22:57,860 --> 00:22:59,000
Well, they're going to have to put it together.

381
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:00,820
Exactly what does that mean?

382
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:04,480
Well, they'll have to make the phone calls.

383
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:07,860
They'll have to start meeting once a month, make a schedule.

384
00:23:08,100 --> 00:23:15,740
They'll have to have a planning conference and put together a schedule, when they're going to meet, when the venture patrol is going to meet, how much money do they need.

385
00:23:16,220 --> 00:23:19,400
You know, they'll have to lay all that out, and that's what they did.

386
00:23:19,480 --> 00:23:25,480
Now, eventually it comes down to somebody writing a check from the committee or using a credit card.

387
00:23:25,480 --> 00:23:32,860
I mean, you know, and then somebody has to actually drive, you know, there's a point at which, you know, they can't go past.

388
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:34,720
They're actually putting the thing together.

389
00:23:34,840 --> 00:23:35,520
That's up to them.

390
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:45,140
My experience has been is once a group of scouts do actually put something together, then it becomes more of a self-perpetuating thing.

391
00:23:45,140 --> 00:23:47,520
It takes on a life of its own.

392
00:23:48,100 --> 00:23:48,280
Yes.

393
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:51,580
And the next group says, well, we want to do that, too.

394
00:23:52,060 --> 00:23:52,440
Right.

395
00:23:52,600 --> 00:23:54,680
And they've got a bit of a path to follow.

396
00:23:55,020 --> 00:23:59,740
It's just that bushwhacking in the first time around that can be a little bit of work.

397
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:07,660
I don't think guys like or appreciate people that aren't sure where they're going or what they're doing.

398
00:24:07,660 --> 00:24:18,480
And I think that, you know, latching on to the program and finding areas to be very, very consistent can do a lot for you.

399
00:24:18,700 --> 00:24:20,580
I just think that's very, very important.

400
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:28,000
Trying to listen to everybody and make changes based on what every scout or every committee member says.

401
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:29,060
Don't go there.

402
00:24:29,720 --> 00:24:32,620
Decide what the program is and then do it.

403
00:24:33,100 --> 00:24:35,140
Now, that doesn't mean you can't take suggestions.

404
00:24:35,260 --> 00:24:36,060
I'm not saying that.

405
00:24:36,060 --> 00:24:46,120
But if you're spending a lot of time waffling, listening to a lot of people, things are confused, I think you just need to figure it out and get consistent.

406
00:24:47,560 --> 00:24:49,840
And I see some of that.

407
00:24:50,340 --> 00:24:55,620
And then another thing successful is emphasize the scout oath and the scout law.

408
00:24:55,720 --> 00:24:56,780
Every opportunity.

409
00:24:57,280 --> 00:24:59,659
Scout Master Minute, conferences, courts of honor.

410
00:25:00,040 --> 00:25:04,220
You should be saying, you know, trustworthy, loyal, helpful, da-da-da-da-da.

411
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:05,780
You should be saying that every week.

412
00:25:06,060 --> 00:25:07,179
Every camp out.

413
00:25:07,580 --> 00:25:10,240
Somewhere that should come up all the time.

414
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:15,139
Explain scouting to somebody who's just taken over a leadership position.

415
00:25:15,940 --> 00:25:18,820
I tell any new parent, any new person coming in the troop.

416
00:25:18,879 --> 00:25:21,060
There's three people in charge of the troop.

417
00:25:21,700 --> 00:25:24,500
The SPL, me, and the committee chairman.

418
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:25,860
And we do everything.

419
00:25:26,280 --> 00:25:29,780
The three of us are responsible for every single thing that happens.

420
00:25:29,780 --> 00:25:33,000
The point is, is that you're there to serve.

421
00:25:33,300 --> 00:25:35,200
So start off serving everything.

422
00:25:35,800 --> 00:25:39,240
And let people come in and find out where they want to serve.

423
00:25:39,720 --> 00:25:41,980
Now, assistant scout masters are slightly different.

424
00:25:42,379 --> 00:25:44,740
If you're going to come do that, you've got to go get trained.

425
00:25:44,740 --> 00:25:47,460
What value do you put on training?

426
00:25:47,460 --> 00:25:54,060
For Boy Scout leaders, scout masters, and assistant scout masters, up to IOLS, it's absolutely essential.

427
00:25:54,620 --> 00:25:56,660
They have to do all the online stuff.

428
00:25:56,760 --> 00:25:58,460
They have to go to scout master training.

429
00:25:58,800 --> 00:26:02,500
And they have to take the outdoor leadership training.

430
00:26:02,920 --> 00:26:04,560
Preferably in the first year they're there.

431
00:26:04,920 --> 00:26:05,920
Get all of that done.

432
00:26:07,160 --> 00:26:08,560
That's vitally important.

433
00:26:08,560 --> 00:26:13,899
Let's say I'm interested in being a scout leader, but I'm a little skeptical about the training.

434
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,860
And I'm wondering if it's going to be worth my time.

435
00:26:18,159 --> 00:26:19,520
Give me, sell me on it.

436
00:26:19,580 --> 00:26:20,720
Why is it worth my time?

437
00:26:21,100 --> 00:26:26,100
The training helps you to learn about the program from multiple people.

438
00:26:26,320 --> 00:26:29,800
And they need to find out what scouting is all about.

439
00:26:30,040 --> 00:26:31,919
They need to meet the district executive.

440
00:26:32,600 --> 00:26:34,840
They need to know there's a council out there.

441
00:26:34,840 --> 00:26:39,480
And they just kind of need to get a feel for all of that.

442
00:26:39,699 --> 00:26:40,760
It gives them perspective.

443
00:26:41,540 --> 00:26:47,120
And then they get to also understand the network of assistants that's there for them.

444
00:26:47,879 --> 00:26:50,540
It just doesn't begin and end with a unit.

445
00:26:51,720 --> 00:26:54,320
And interact with the other students in the classes.

446
00:26:54,699 --> 00:26:56,320
I think that's very, very valuable.

447
00:26:56,699 --> 00:26:58,980
Tell me what your most memorable scouting moment is.

448
00:26:59,100 --> 00:27:00,240
It's kind of two moments.

449
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:03,620
When I was 14 years old, I think I was about almost 15,

450
00:27:03,620 --> 00:27:09,979
I remember standing in the pouring down rain on top of Thunderhead Mountain in the middle of Smoky Mountains National Park.

451
00:27:10,219 --> 00:27:18,760
We were a little less than 30 miles into a 50-miler hike that we had totally underestimated.

452
00:27:19,979 --> 00:27:22,120
And we had to make some decisions.

453
00:27:22,320 --> 00:27:24,459
So we decided to hike off the mountain.

454
00:27:24,760 --> 00:27:26,459
And it ended up being a great trip.

455
00:27:26,800 --> 00:27:31,060
But there's always that little bit of disappointment that you didn't quite make the 50-miler.

456
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:37,260
Well, the other part of that is in 1991, we planned a 50-miler in the Appalachian Trail.

457
00:27:37,700 --> 00:27:44,620
I came up the last of the trail, the last half a mile coming up Roan Mountain from the south is very vertical.

458
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:47,260
My son Christopher was at the top of the hill.

459
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:49,560
He was the kids.

460
00:27:49,740 --> 00:27:51,860
The mooses were way ahead of us old guys.

461
00:27:51,860 --> 00:28:02,500
But I just remember that picture very vividly in my mind, stepping up to the last little step onto the flats and thinking, you know, I finally finished that 50-miler.

462
00:28:02,899 --> 00:28:03,199
Wow.

463
00:28:03,300 --> 00:28:04,959
And I did it with my son Christopher.

464
00:28:05,500 --> 00:28:08,020
To me, that was just really cool.

465
00:28:08,219 --> 00:28:10,399
A lot of things came out of that over the years.

466
00:28:10,760 --> 00:28:14,580
But finishing that with Christopher was a real high point.

467
00:28:14,580 --> 00:28:28,879
I think everybody's most memorable moment in scouting involves rain or thunder or wind or, you know, it's the other 100 campouts that you go on that are nice and sunny and bright.

468
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:30,659
They just all seem to mold together.

469
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:34,820
It's the ones that put some challenge in front of you that are really memorable.

470
00:28:35,699 --> 00:28:37,139
That's – you remember those.

471
00:28:37,140 --> 00:28:42,220
There's another memory I have that's happened multiple times.

472
00:28:42,280 --> 00:28:50,400
Quite a few times I've had moms come up to me and tell me after summer camp, three weeks ago my son was a boy.

473
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:54,240
The last week there's another man in my house.

474
00:28:54,660 --> 00:28:55,040
Uh-huh.

475
00:28:55,180 --> 00:28:56,840
They're just totally blown away.

476
00:28:57,120 --> 00:28:57,940
It is amazing.

477
00:28:58,220 --> 00:29:01,480
When they tell me that, I just – I don't know.

478
00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:02,840
It's just a really cool thing.

479
00:29:03,400 --> 00:29:04,960
Makes it worth it.

480
00:29:05,600 --> 00:29:05,920
Yes.

481
00:29:05,920 --> 00:29:06,380
Yeah.

482
00:29:07,140 --> 00:29:07,580
Absolutely.

483
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:09,860
Makes all the rain and the wind and the – yeah.

484
00:29:10,520 --> 00:29:12,900
I sure do appreciate you talking with me, Larry.

485
00:29:13,100 --> 00:29:24,260
I think any time people can talk scouting and encourage others as they're meeting the same challenges that we all do, that it's a great encouragement to them.

486
00:29:24,680 --> 00:29:25,160
So –

487
00:29:25,160 --> 00:29:29,160
You know, I don't know whether you think about it or not, but you are on the cutting edge.

488
00:29:29,620 --> 00:29:34,540
You know, this is a whole new thing and you are – you know, you're right out there in front of it.

489
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:42,680
And I think experienced people like yourself, just as an example, need to be out there, you know, saying what you're saying.

490
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:46,840
I appreciate your encouragement and it's been a good time talking to you.

491
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:47,920
It's winter –

492
00:29:47,920 --> 00:29:49,060
My wife wants to listen to it.

493
